This page sets up the self-hosted editor for editors who are not developers. It is one Docker container: the image `ghcr.io/amuedespacher/micacms`. If you only edit on your own machine, you do not need this page. Use [Getting started](/docs/getting-started/).

> Mica is pre-1.0. Test upgrades and keep the version fixed in production.

## What you need

- A server with Docker and Docker Compose, and a reverse proxy that gives you HTTPS. Example: `https://cms.example.com`.
- A content repository on GitHub or GitLab. It must contain the content model at `content/.mica/schema.json`. Your Astro build writes that file, and you commit it.
- An OpenID Connect provider. Mica keeps no passwords. If you have none, use Google: [Sign in with Google](/docs/sign-in-with-google/).

Self-hosted Mica publishes only through GitHub or GitLab. It never runs local Git. `REPOSITORY=local` stops the start-up with an error. To edit a checkout on your machine, use the local editor.

## 1. Get the two files

You do not clone the Mica repository. The image is published.

```bash
mkdir -p /srv/mica && cd /srv/mica
curl -o compose.yml https://raw.githubusercontent.com/micacms/mica/main/deploy/self-hosted/compose.yml
curl -o .env https://raw.githubusercontent.com/micacms/mica/main/deploy/self-hosted/.env.example
```

## 2. Connect the repository

Mica commits through the provider API. Make a token for that one repository.

For GitHub, make a fine-grained personal access token with **Contents: read and write**. For GitLab, make a project access token with the `write_repository` scope.

```env
REPOSITORY=github
REPOSITORY_PROJECT=acme/website
REPOSITORY_TOKEN=github_pat_...
REPOSITORY_ROOT=content
REPOSITORY_BRANCH=main
```

For GitLab, set `REPOSITORY=gitlab`. `REPOSITORY_PROJECT` is `group/project` or the numeric id.

`REPOSITORY_ROOT` defaults to `content`. `REPOSITORY_BRANCH` defaults to `main`. Mica reads and writes only below the root, even if the token can write more. For GitHub Enterprise Server or a self-managed GitLab, set `REPOSITORY_API_BASE`.

## 3. Connect the sign-in

Mica asks an OpenID Connect provider who a person is.

```env
MICA_OIDC_ISSUER=https://auth.example.com
MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=mica
MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
```

- `MICA_OIDC_ISSUER` must equal the `issuer` that the provider publishes at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`.
- Omit `MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET` for a public client that uses PKCE.
- Register one redirect URI with the provider: `<MICA_PUBLIC_URL>/api/auth/callback`.
- Mica asks for the scopes `openid profile email`. PKCE, state, and nonce are verified.

Step by step: [Sign in with Google](/docs/sign-in-with-google/) or [Other sign-in providers](/docs/other-providers/).

## 4. Say who may edit

Your provider knows many people. It does not know who may edit this website. Set at least one rule. Mica refuses to start without one.

```env
MICA_ADMIT_GROUP=mica-editors
MICA_ADMIT_DOMAIN=example.com
MICA_ADMIT_EMAILS=alice@gmail.com,bob@example.com
MICA_ADMIT_SUBJECTS=https://auth.example.com|a1b2c3
```

Each rule is a comma-separated list. One rule is enough.

- **Group:** the usual choice. Put a person in the group at your provider to add an editor. The claim name is `groups`. If your provider uses another name, set `MICA_OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM`. A Keycloak path such as `/mica-editors` and the bare name both match.
- **Domain:** every address in a domain, but only addresses that the provider verified. Mica refuses a public domain such as `gmail.com` at start-up.
- **Emails:** these addresses, if the provider verified them. The comparison ignores case. Use this rule for Google accounts. One cost: if an organisation gives the address of a former editor to a new person, that person gets access. Gmail never reuses an address.
- **Subjects:** `issuer|subject` pairs. This is the strictest rule. A subject is never reassigned.

Everybody that a rule admits is an editor. There are no roles, no user table, and no invitations.

## 5. Set the address and the secret

```env
MICA_PUBLIC_URL=https://cms.example.com
TRUST_PROXY=true
SESSION_SECRET=
```

- `MICA_PUBLIC_URL` must be exactly what the browser shows. `https://` turns on Secure cookies. Mica refuses plain-HTTP discovery in production.
- Set `TRUST_PROXY=true` only behind a proxy that you control.
- `SESSION_SECRET` must have at least 32 characters. Make one:

```bash
node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
```

The container listens on `127.0.0.1:6422`. Put TLS in front of it. With Caddy, this is enough:

```text
cms.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:6422
}
```

## 6. Start and check

```bash
docker compose up -d
docker compose logs mica
curl -s https://cms.example.com/health
```

You should see a start-up line that names the rule in force, and `/health` should answer with `"status": "ok"`. Open `https://cms.example.com/admin/` and sign in.

If the container stops, read the log. Mica stops at start-up when `REPOSITORY` is not `github` or `gitlab`, when the project or the token is missing, when no admission rule is set, or when the repository has no content model at `.mica/schema.json`.

## Drafts and the data volume

A save is a draft. Drafts are in a SQLite file in the `/data` volume. Only editors see them. **Publish** writes all pending drafts as one commit. The push starts your site build, and the website build needs no CMS address and no token.

The volume holds unpublished drafts only. Never the website. If you lose it, unpublished work is gone. Nobody is signed out, and everything published is in the repository.

## Sessions and removal of access

A session is an encrypted cookie. Mica keeps no list of sessions.

```env
SESSION_TTL_HOURS=8
SESSION_IDLE_HOURS=4
```

- A session ends after 8 hours, or after 4 hours without use. While a session is in use, Mica issues the cookie again every 5 minutes.
- Sign-out clears the cookie in the browser only.
- Mica re-reads the rules on each request. The rules live in `.env`, so restart the container after a change. The change then counts at once.
- A change at your provider, for example removal from a group, counts from the next sign-in.
- To remove a person at once, take them out of the rules and restart. To end all sessions, change `SESSION_SECRET` and restart.

## Backups

The repository is the content backup, with the history of every publish. For unpublished work, back up the `/data` volume. Use the SQLite `.backup` command and not a plain copy.

## Upgrades

The image tag in `compose.yml` is the version. Change it, then run:

```bash
docker compose pull && docker compose up -d
```

Read the changelog first, and pin a version.

When you change the content model, run the Astro build, commit `.mica/schema.json`, push, and restart:

```bash
docker compose restart mica
```

## Next

Sign in with a Google account: [Sign in with Google](/docs/sign-in-with-google/). Use another provider: [Other sign-in providers](/docs/other-providers/).
